<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kosrae paramount chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1874 CE (approx.) · capital Leluh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The island-wide chiefdom of Kosrae in Micronesia, ruled from the basalt-walled royal town of Leluh, a sister site to Pohnpei's Nan Madol.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Between about 1250 and 1400 the chiefdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosrae">Kosrae</a>, the easternmost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a>, were probably unified under a single paramount ruler, the Tokosra. The capital was built on Leluh, a small island off the east coast that was enlarged with coral fill and covered with walled compounds of stacked basalt columns over the following centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosrae">Kosrae</a> was among the most centralised societies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micronesia">Micronesia</a>. The Tokosra held ultimate title to land, and high chiefs living at Leluh administered the island's districts, drawing food from commoners who farmed breadfruit, taro and bananas on the main island. Royal tombs stood inside high walls, and canals ran through the town. Pohnpeian tradition makes Kosrae the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isokelekel">Isokelekel</a>, who overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saudeleur-dynasty">Saudeleur dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Whalers, beginning in the 1830s, brought epidemics that reduced the population to a few hundred by the 1870s. American Protestant missionaries arrived in 1852, and the old kingship lost its authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosrae">Kosrae</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1885, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1899 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1914, and is now a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1250 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.58%;width:12.42%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leluh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Kosraean traditional religion, later Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kosraean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Micronesia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Leluh, Kingdom of Kosrae</dd></dl></aside></div>

